description:
Consider the following rmarkdown excerpt

```{r}
library(slickR)
```
'# Section 1 {.tabset .tabset-fade .tabset-pills}

'## Subsection A {-}

```{r,chunk1}
slickR( imgs1, ... ) + settings( ... )
```
'## Subsection B {-}

```{r,chunk2}
slickR( imgs2, ... ) + settings( ... )
```

As it is well known, the slider in chunk2 will first render hidden, therefore, when the user selects that tab, the slider will appear empty (the desired behavior would be for it to appear with imgs2). This is a FAQ, but what do I need to do in a separate .css to intialize the hidden sliders?

For a live example do check out this [page](https://dan-reznik.github.io/Elliptical-Billiards-Triangular-Orbits/envelopes1618.html). Under "Envelope Carousels" check the 2nd tab called "X(2) Barycenter" and notice the slider appears empty.

rchk seems to have a problem with this section of code:

https://github.com/DavisVaughan/slide/blob/21e2e41310063e649b1a96c63781e8fbb39ebc85/src/index.c#L96-L114

It seems to think the `UNPROTECT(2 + recycled);` is incorrect, although I think that that is a false alarm. The error I get is:

```r
#> Function slide_index_common_impl
#> [PB] uses multiple pointer protection counters results will be incomplete
#> [UP] unsupported form of unprotect, unprotecting all variables, results will be incomplete /opt/R-svn/packages/build/ZkkHRHeM/slide/src/index.c:113
```

Replacing the chunk with a `n_prot_elt` counter like this doesn't help either, I still got the error

```c
int n_prot_elt = 0;

elt = PROTECT_N(vctrs_cast(elt, ptype, strings_empty, strings_empty), &n_prot_elt);
elt = PROTECT_N(vec_proxy(elt), &n_prot_elt);

R_len_t elt_size = vec_size(elt);

if (elt_size != 1) {
  stop_not_all_size_one(i + 1, elt_size);
}

if (out_index_size != 1) {
  elt = PROTECT_N(vec_recycle(elt, out_index_size), &n_prot_elt);
}

vec_assign_impl(out, out_index, elt, false);
UNPROTECT(n_prot_elt);
```

Maybe if we _always_ `PROTECT()` the result of `vec_recycle()` rather than conditionally protect it then it will be ok?
